WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.711
When you picture a unicorn,
what comes to mind?

00:00:02.794 --> 00:00:04.421
A white horse with
a gleaming horn,

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.631
maybe a shaggy rhinoceros,

00:00:06.715 --> 00:00:10.260
a terrifying horned rabbit
or a multicolored chimera?

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:13.722
The tragic news is that only
one of these creatures is real

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:16.850
but the good news is that
all of them are unicorns.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:19.644
Before unicorns were cemented
as the universal symbol

00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:22.021
of purity and grace
we picture today,

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:25.150
they were a whole malleable
class of folklore creatures

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:26.651
who shared a single trait:

00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:28.903
that solitary horn
erupting from their head.

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:31.114
Most of the modern
unicorn's predecessors

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.657
had dark dispositions

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:35.201
and body parts borrowed
from multiple beasts,

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:38.163
likely inspired by
real-world animal anatomy.

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:40.582
Thus hidden in the
mythology of the unicorn

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:43.418
is a lesson in how humans
collective imagination

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:46.963
can adapt truth into whatever
fable we need at the time.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:50.008
[inquisitive music]

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:54.679
Since time immemorial,
one-horned creatures

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:57.140
have prowled our dreams
and nightmares alike,

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.058
but they didn't
spring fully formed

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:00.727
from myths or fairy tales.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:04.606
Instead, we find powerful
unicorns in natural histories,

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:07.525
travel logs, and geography
from around the world.

00:01:07.609 --> 00:01:10.361
For this reason, many
unicorns straddle the boundary

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:12.322
between fact and fiction.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.741
And that only becomes
more obvious when we look

00:01:14.824 --> 00:01:17.577
at how they've been translated
across different cultures.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.288
While some one-horned
creatures influenced the figure

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:21.998
of the white equine unicorn

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.209
that became popular in
the Western imagination,

00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:26.795
they also complicate
its symbolism.

00:01:26.878 --> 00:01:29.422
Rather than denoting
purity or chastity,

00:01:29.506 --> 00:01:31.257
the unicorns we'll
talk about today

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:34.677
are hybrid creatures
with multifaceted powers.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.054
The millennials in
the audience know

00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:37.806
who "The Last Unicorn" is

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:40.391
but one of the first
is the bovine unicorn

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:43.269
of the Indus Valley
civilization in South Asia.

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:47.690
Described as a bull, ox ,or
an antelope of heavy build

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:50.151
with a horn protruding
from the back of its head.

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:54.781
It's been found on official
seals dating to 2,600 BCE.

00:01:54.864 --> 00:01:56.950
Figurines of this creature
have also been found

00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:00.537
across ancient India,
Pakistan, and Mesopotamia.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.748
Some scholars have linked these
stocky single-horned figures

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:06.751
to the very real elasmotherium,
a giant rhinoceros

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:10.004
that had already
been extinct for eons

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:13.007
and whose fossils could have
inspired the unicorn myth.

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:14.551
Whatever the inspiration,

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.803
the Indus unicorn was
usually shown beside

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:18.763
a stand of sacred water
or other offerings

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:22.225
indicating that this
is no ordinary animal.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.811
Another exceptional creature
continues this trend

00:02:24.894 --> 00:02:28.857
of fusing zoology and
mythology, the Chinese qilin.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.901
It's described as having
the body of a deer or elk,

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.779
the tail of an ox
and a single horn,

00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:36.865
but also shows up
covered in scales

00:02:36.948 --> 00:02:39.492
and surrounded by
fire like a dragon.

00:02:39.576 --> 00:02:41.828
Qilin were known for
their gentle disposition,

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:43.580
and as tokens of good luck

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.456
with their appearance
often heralding

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.375
the birth of an
important person.

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:49.168
While some accounts
picture the qilin

00:02:49.252 --> 00:02:51.963
as a supernatural figure
who clomps across the earth

00:02:52.046 --> 00:02:54.340
without disturbing
a single blade of grass,

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:56.509
they also made
their way into real life.

00:02:56.593 --> 00:03:00.179
In 1414, the Ming emperor
was presented with a qilin

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:02.765
from a far away land
only to discover

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:06.060
that what he really had on
his hands was a giraffe.

00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:07.645
Amalgamations of mythical

00:03:07.729 --> 00:03:09.689
and real animals
continue in the karkadann

00:03:09.772 --> 00:03:13.067
which appears in
Islamic writing and art.

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:15.194
Described by polymath al-Biruni

00:03:15.278 --> 00:03:19.032
as a scaly buffalo with yellow
hooves and a horned nose,

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:20.450
scholars generally agree

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.577
that he was talking
about rhinos here,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.038
but embellishments
and mistranslations

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:27.665
produce karkadanns
as winged antelopes,

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:30.543
horses,
and even feline figures.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:32.378
Others fuse different animals.

00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:35.006
In one account, the karkadann
sports the head of a camel,

00:03:35.089 --> 00:03:38.343
body of a mule, and the
tail and hooves of a bull.

00:03:38.426 --> 00:03:41.512
In another, it's a giant
elephant horse mashup.

00:03:41.596 --> 00:03:44.349
The karkadanns horn was also
the source of much debate.

00:03:44.432 --> 00:03:47.060
The medieval geographer
Muhammed al-Idrisi tells us

00:03:47.143 --> 00:03:50.229
that it's so big you can't
fit both your hands around it.

00:03:50.313 --> 00:03:52.357
While the naturalist
al-Damiri insists

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.275
that the horn is so heavy

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:56.778
the karkadann can't even
hold its own head up.

00:03:56.861 --> 00:04:00.073
In all their forms,
karkadanns were fearsome.

00:04:00.156 --> 00:04:02.408
They were said to
actively hunt humans

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:05.453
or to inflict death on
all who heard their voice.

00:04:05.536 --> 00:04:08.414
It also has a fraught
relationship with other animals.

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:11.709
In one story, the karkadann
takes out its mortal enemy,

00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:14.545
the elephant, by impaling
the elephant so deeply

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:16.464
that it can't extract its horn.

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:19.258
This story tends to end
in pretty grizzly ways

00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:21.135
with all the elephant's
fat seeping out

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:23.388
and blinding the
karkadann, for instance,

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:26.432
or a huge bird carrying
both animals to her nest

00:04:26.516 --> 00:04:28.101
and feeding them to her chicks.

00:04:28.184 --> 00:04:31.145
These stories convey the
brutality of the animal realm

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:33.481
which the karkadann
appeared to rule over,

00:04:33.564 --> 00:04:35.650
but never with
absolute security.

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:37.902
In comparison to
other Islamic unicorns

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:40.780
the shadhavar seems
deceptively benign.

00:04:40.863 --> 00:04:42.031
This deer-like animal

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.450
sprouts hollow branches
from its single horn

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:47.704
which makes sweet music as
the wind whistles through them

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:50.707
but the music ultimately
attracts unsuspecting animals

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.002
who are promptly devoured
by the wily shadhavar.

00:04:54.085 --> 00:04:56.170
These dangerous tendencies
don't stop people

00:04:56.254 --> 00:04:57.797
from coveting their horns.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.757
So-called karkadann horns,

00:04:59.841 --> 00:05:02.218
whose original owner
was usually a rhino,

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:05.221
was sold as a remedy
for countless illnesses.

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:07.890
It was said that a karkadann
horn could also be used

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.685
to heat cold water,
ward off evil forces,

00:05:10.768 --> 00:05:12.979
and keep horses
standing upright.

00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:14.480
In one Persian legend,

00:05:14.564 --> 00:05:16.524
karkadann horn
is mixed with dust

00:05:16.607 --> 00:05:18.818
and sprinkled over
a band of robbers,

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:21.821
immobilizing them before
they can ransack a village.

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:23.281
These Middle Eastern unicorns

00:05:23.364 --> 00:05:25.324
look very different
from each other,

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:27.076
but are united by their rarity,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.620
ferocity, and unpredictability.

00:05:29.704 --> 00:05:31.873
Descriptions of these
creatures reveal the overlap

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:34.125
between myth and observation

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:35.793
in that they tend to
embellish the animal realm

00:05:35.877 --> 00:05:39.172
and heighten the chaos
of the natural world.

00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:41.674
Early Western ideas about
what the unicorn looked like

00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:44.510
echo this embellishment,
but they also stemmed

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:48.389
from confusion and intrigue
about exotic, faraway creatures.

00:05:48.473 --> 00:05:49.766
In a tangled zoological

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:52.602
and geographical
report of "The East,"

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:55.980
the Greek physician Ctesias
reported Indian unicorns

00:05:56.064 --> 00:06:00.109
with crimson heads,
multi-colored horns,
and shapely ankles.

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:03.404
He also notes their
blood-thirsty violent nature.

00:06:03.488 --> 00:06:07.283
Pliny wrote of monokeroses,
meaning single horn,

00:06:07.366 --> 00:06:10.161
who each had a stag's
head, elephant's feet,

00:06:10.244 --> 00:06:13.247
a boar's tail, and
a single black horn.

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:16.501
While Claudius Aelianus
described a red-haired horse

00:06:16.584 --> 00:06:19.754
with a curly pigtail
and spiraling horn.

00:06:19.837 --> 00:06:21.798
These creatures have
been variously linked

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.467
to the Indian rhino,
the Tibetan antelope

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.346
and the onager, or kiang,
both a type of donkey.

00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:30.807
But Europeans had their
own take on the unicorn

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.518
throughout the early
Christian and Medieval period.

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:35.019
Christian writers
linked unicorns

00:06:35.103 --> 00:06:36.521
and their mystical powers

00:06:36.604 --> 00:06:40.024
to Jesus Christ himself
or a symbol of the cross.

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:41.567
The unicorn was thought

00:06:41.651 --> 00:06:43.820
to have kept Adam and Eve
Company in the Garden of Eden

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:46.447
cementing its status
as supreme animal

00:06:46.531 --> 00:06:48.491
and a spiritual allegory.

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:51.410
So as unicorns were absorbed
into Christian iconography,

00:06:51.494 --> 00:06:55.289
they became associated with
purity, chastity, and unity.

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:56.582
There's a clear distinction

00:06:56.666 --> 00:06:58.459
between the pure,
angelic unicorn

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:01.087
and its hybrid
fearsome predecessors.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:04.298
Recently, the unicorn has
earned a third reputation

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:05.675
as we in the queer community

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:08.177
have claimed its wild
side for ourselves.

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:09.846
There are plenty
of glittery horns

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:11.848
and flowing rainbow manes
in queer iconography

00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:14.725
but the connection
goes deeper than that.

00:07:14.809 --> 00:07:17.145
In queer culture,
unicorns are celebrated

00:07:17.228 --> 00:07:19.480
as creatures that
shun the status quo

00:07:19.564 --> 00:07:22.650
while reminding us how
special and resilient we are.

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:25.862
We have adopted the unicorn
as slang and symbol,

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:28.072
empowering ourselves
and our queer kin

00:07:28.156 --> 00:07:29.866
to embrace our identities.

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:32.785
LGBTQ+ soldiers fighting
in Ukraine's army

00:07:32.869 --> 00:07:34.745
have started sewing
unicorn patches

00:07:34.829 --> 00:07:38.624
onto their uniforms as a way
to express pride, solidarity

00:07:38.708 --> 00:07:40.251
and the desire to be seen.

00:07:40.334 --> 00:07:41.711
Here we see echoes of older

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:44.338
and more varied
understandings of the unicorn

00:07:44.422 --> 00:07:47.008
whose power lies in
its outsider status,

00:07:47.091 --> 00:07:49.635
its hybridity and its
resistance to classification.

00:07:49.719 --> 00:07:52.930
Ultimately, it's this
lack of consensus

00:07:53.014 --> 00:07:55.391
that gives unicorns
their symbolic power.

00:07:55.474 --> 00:07:58.603
As creatures who muddy the
line between fact and fiction,

00:07:58.686 --> 00:08:00.521
disrupt cultural boundaries,

00:08:00.605 --> 00:08:03.524
and reveal fears and fantasies
about the natural world,

00:08:03.608 --> 00:08:07.403
unicorns have always been about
subjective interpretation.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:10.531
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

